our story begins in the summer of 1940.  the people of western europe were in a  profound state of shock  in just a few weeks nazi germany with  blinding speed  had invaded and occupied six countries  knocking them over like dominoes  one of them was belgium seventeen days  after german troops  poured over belgian's border its  government surrendered  but the hero of our story did not  surrender  her name is andre dejong she was known  as didi in the belgian capital of  brussels  didi's father a school master named paul  de jong  burst into tears when he told his 23  year old daughter  about the government's capitulation she  had never seen her father cry before  and it made her not only sad but very  angry  not at him but at the germans she told  her father  you are wrong to cry you'll see what we  do to them  her spirit of defiance was very unusual  in occupied europe in those early days  of world war  ii most europeans were still  totally overwhelmed by what had just  happened  they had no idea how to react much less  fight back at that point the german  occupiers  seemed all-powerful the main goal of  most people under their control was  simply to survive  but that was not good enough for didi  dejong  from the very beginning she was  determined to resist  just a year after belgium's occupation  she had become one of the top resistance  leaders in europe  she began her work in brussels shortly  after the belgian surrender  these are the kind of people i'm going  to focus on in these lectures  people i've encountered in my research  who are unsung heroes  who made a difference in world war ii  but who are unknown to most people  and who have never been given the credit  they deserved  several of them in addition to dede are  women  dede was a commercial artist by  profession but when belgium was invaded  she started working as a nurse for  british troops wounded in the fighting  there  along with a group of friends and  acquaintances she began to smuggle  injured british soldiers out of  german-controlled hospitals  and take them to nearby safe houses that  she had set up  not long afterwards she traveled  secretly to spain  which during world war ii was a neutral  country  there she met with british officials she  told them that many british servicemen  were still hiding in belgium  some were soldiers others were airmen  shot down on recent bombing missions  in the past few months she said she  along with several friends of hers had  set up an escape line through belgium  and france  to help these men get back to england  if the british government would give her  money to help pay for the line  for guides safe houses food and train  fares  she could bring out these men but she  made clear  that the escape line would have to  remain under her control  at first the british were skeptical it  seemed unlikely to them that this petite  slender young woman  had traveled from german-occupied  belgium  through occupied france and over the  dizzying heights of the pyrenees  mountains to spain  eventually however they verified her  story  and agreed to finance her operation  that gamble paid off in a big way  by the time the war was over dijon's  escape network  which was called the comet line would be  the largest  and most important escape line in  occupied europe  it would be credited with rescuing more  than 800 british and american servicemen  getting them out of enemy territory and  back to freedom  there were a number of other networks  like the comet line throughout europe  but they were most active in three  countries  belgium france and holland  starting in 1941 allied bombers flew  over these countries  on their daily bombing missions between  england and germany  a sizable number of the planes were shot  down during those flights  but when they were many crew members  were able to parachute out before the  planes crashed  which left them stranded behind enemy  lines  at a time when trained bomber crews were  in desperately short supply  it was vital for the allied war effort  to rescue as  many of these airmen as possible and  bring them back to england  to continue the fight the comet line  worked mostly in belgium and france the  headquarters of the line was in brussels  and then it snaked its way south through  france to the pyrenees mountains  which lay on the border of france and  spain  it worked somewhat like an assembly line  stranded allied airmen  when they were found were taken to the  closest safe house  which usually was in a village or on a  farm  then they were taken to collection  points in brussels and paris  from there they were escorted hundreds  of miles by comet line workers  to a chain of safe houses in the  foothills of the pyrenees  from that point spanish guides escorted  them over the mountains to spain  and from there they returned to england  members of the comet line network came  from a wide variety of backgrounds and  occupations  ranging from students to aristocrats to  factory workers  many of them were in their teens and  twenties  and as was true for other escape lines  the majority of comet line workers  couriers guides and operators of safe  houses  were women being part of an escape  network  was probably the most dangerous form of  resistance work  in europe german officials were keenly  aware of the value of these airmen to  the allied bombing effort  and they ordered the escape lines to be  crushed  using any means necessary  for escape line workers the danger of  capture was  ever present and the penalty for their  work was  far more severe than that faced by the  men they were hiding  if allied servicemen were captured they  were sent to prisoner  of war camps where geneva convention  rules applied  but when escape line members were caught  they faced torture  the horrors of a nazi concentration camp  and or execution  it was particularly dangerous for the  couriers  most of them young women many of them  still in their teens  who escorted the servicemen hundreds of  miles across  enemy territory these women did their  work  out in the open riding on trains and  other forms of public transportation  with young men who clearly were not  european  as one british intelligence officer  observed  it is not an easy matter to hide a  foreigner in your midst  especially when it happens to be a  red-haired scotsman  or a gum-chewing american from the  middle west finding clothes to fit  americans who tended to be considerably  taller than britons or other europeans  was a particular problem so was finding  enough food  at a time when food was increasingly  scarce for everyone  in europe sometimes american airmen on  the run  had a hard time understanding why they  were given so little to eat  that was because they were coming from a  country that had very little experience  dealing with the shortages caused by war  british airmen were less demanding  because their own country had been  strictly rationed  since 1940. british airmen were also  considered calmer and more disciplined  than their american counterparts who  were more independent  and outgoing and sometimes less willing  to accept the orders of these young  women  who were guiding them to freedom before  they set out  on these extremely dangerous journeys  comet line workers did their best to  teach the young men they were guiding  how to blend in while in public  in one case a bowlegged texan was  instructed in the fine art  of walking like a european since most of  the airmen spoke only english  they were ordered to keep quiet at all  times in public  american airmen were told to keep their  hands out of their pockets  which was a particularly american trait  and they were coached on the way  europeans use silverware when eating  keeping their fork in their left hand  rather than shifting it back and forth  like americans do during a meal  both britain's and americans were told  never to smoke cigarettes produced in  their own countries  the smoke had an odor significantly  different  from that of european cigarettes and  they could never be seen with a bar of  chocolate  which was impossible to find in occupied  europe during the war  as the men were repeatedly told the  slightest mistake could draw the  attention of one of the many  french belgian and german security  officials  who patrolled buses trains and other  forms of public transportation  after the war the airmen who were saved  by members of the comet line  spoke very movingly about the  selflessness and courage of their  rescuers  one of them was an englishman named bob  frost  who as a nineteen-year-old sergeant was  escorted to  spain by didi dejong herself in the  autumn of 1942.  speaking of dede and her colleagues  frost said  i fell in love with them totally  absolutely  they knew the price if they were caught  it was heroism beyond anything i can  tell you  when the airmen returned to england they  were questioned by  officials from mi9 which was a small  clandestine government agency  created to help rescue allied servicemen  trapped behind enemy lines  mi9 was run by two young british army  officers  james langley and arie neve who knew  from personal experience what these men  had gone through  both langley and neve had fought against  the germans in france  during the late spring of 1940 both had  been stranded behind enemy lines  and both had been helped by newly formed  escape networks  when they returned to england they were  asked to join mi9  which provided money and radio  communications to those involved  in running the escape lines  when langley and neve questioned  returning airmen about their escape  experiences  they recalled how these men's eyes  filled with tears  when talking about deedee and her  associates  arie neve remembered how afraid they  were for her  he and langley felt the same way  they both were well aware that the  bigger an escape line became  the more vulnerable it was it became  virtually impossible to check out the  backgrounds of the hundreds of people  who worked on the escape lines or the  airmen who used them to get back to  england  as a result it's not surprising that the  germans had considerable success  in infiltrating the lines with agents  impersonating allied airmen  most of these impersonators were german  citizens who had lived in britain or the  united states  for long periods and who spoke fluent  english  to help their credibility with the  escape lines they were equipped with  documents  and dog tags that had been recovered  from killed or captured britain's or  americans  the germans were also aided by french  belgian and english collaborators  who joined the lines as workers  knowing all this james langley and erie  neve  wanted to send a radio operator to  brussels to help dede communicate  directly with london  rather than relying on meetings with  british liaison officers in spain  with a radio connection she would have  been able to alert mi9  immediately in case of trouble and mi9  could let her know  about possible traitors and german  agents posing as helpers of allied  airmen  but didi said no right from the  beginning  she had made it clear that while she  would accept financial help from the  british  they would have no say in the actual  operation of the comet line  as langley recalled dede insisted to him  and neve that quote the decisions always  rested with the men  and women in the field the line was  belgian  it would be run by belgians and any help  would be gratefully received  but the payment of money was simply a  reimbursement of expenses  and in no way gave us the right to issue  orders unquote while langley and neve  never stopped worrying about dede they  had to admit  she knew what she was doing she was  involved  in every aspect of the operation she  organized the safe houses  worked with photographers and forgers to  produce false belgian and french  identity papers  and personally guided dozens of airmen  to the spanish frontier  and over the pyrenees she was known  to american and british airmen under her  care as the postmistress  because she referred to them very  affectionately  as her parcels her real identity was  kept a closely guarded secret  from the time of the comet line's  creation in 1941  there had been numerous arrests of its  workers  but deedee herself managed to elude the  nazis for more than 18 months  langley and neve who knew how determined  the germans were to smash the line  were afraid that her luck was running  out  they urged her and her father who worked  closely with her  to come to england before it was too  late  but as always she kept to her own rules  refusing even to consider the thought of  leaving  in early january 1943 didi dejong  set off with three american pilots on  her 18th trip to spain a heavy snowstorm  caught them just before they crossed the  pyrenees  and they were forced to seek shelter for  the night at a safe house in the  foothills of the mountains  the next morning the gestapo acting on a  tip from a local  farm worker burst into the house and  arrested all its occupants  allied airmen were repeatedly warned  that if they were captured  they must not reveal the identities and  locations of those who had hidden or  otherwise helped them  their only obligation they were told was  to give their name  rank and serial number just as if they  had been captured in combat  but the harshness of gestapo  interrogation  proved too much for some including one  of the pilots who was caught with dede  he gave up the names of his guides and  the owners of the safe houses  who had sheltered him while the three  pilots were sent to a pow  camp dede and those who helped her  vanished without a trace  they were among the tens of thousands of  political prisoners in occupied europe  who under a decree from hitler  disappeared into what was  called the knight and fog of nazi  concentration camps  a few weeks later the gestapo again  struck hard at comet  this time arresting nearly 100 of its  operatives in brussels and  elsewhere in belgium before that  happened however  a pretty 28 year old friend and close  associate of deities  named peggy van leer was smuggled out of  belgium  by fellow comet agents and taken to  gibraltar  where she was sent by plane to london  there james langley was waiting to greet  her  he was instantly smitten by the  red-haired blue-eyed belgian  falling in love with her he later told  friends the moment she stepped off the  plane  the two were married the following year  had five children  and lived happily together in suffolk  for the rest of their lives  peggy van leer was lucky to get out when  she did  the first gestapo raid was followed by a  second one  several months later this one triggered  by a french collaborator  working for the gestapo in paris didi  dejong's father  who was head of the line in paris was  rounded up  along with her sister and dozens of  other associates  most of them including paul de jong were  later executed  as james langley later said it seemed  incredible that comet could survive  but survive it did despite the loss of  its founder and so many other key  members  the network was able to rebuild itself  enlisting the help of new couriers and  guides  setting up more safe houses and  maintaining the flow of allied  servicemen back to england  an estimated 3000 civilians mostly  belgians and french  worked with the comet line about 700  were arrested by the germans  and 290 were executed or died in prison  or concentration camps nonetheless  the line was able to continue its rescue  operations until belgium was liberated  in the fall of 1944 of the 800  servicemen  that the comet lines saved in its three  years of existence  118 were escorted to spain  by didi dejong herself  by the end of the war the rescue of  downed allied airmen  had become a commonplace event in all  some seven  thousand british american and other  allied servicemen  were spirited out of occupied europe  during the war  the number of those who helped them  escape has been estimated at more than  12  000 people during the war allied fighter  and bomber crews were briefed on how to  evade capture  and seek out rescuers if they were shot  down  they were given a compass silk maps of  the countries they were flying over  and other emergency supplies to keep  them going while they were looking for  help  if they were shot down they were told  their first objective was to get rid of  their parachute  and leave their landing spot as soon as  possible  as the war progressed those goals became  increasingly easy to achieve  whenever the thunder of planes was heard  in belgium  holland or northern france crowds of  people would gather outside to watch the  planes fly overhead  if parachutes were seen those observers  would hop on their bicycles  or run in the parachute's direction in  the hope of getting there before the  germans  in february 1943 an american pilot shot  down on his way back from a raid on the  french port of san nazaire  was spirited away by several women who  raced up to him  as he landed in august of that year  two american sergeants down near  brussels were each greeted by crowds of  several dozen belgians  and passed on to escape lines that same  month  another u.s sergeant who was shot down  near the french town of toulouse  was swarmed by some 30 frenchmen as he  drifted to the ground  they immediately removed his parachute  and uniform  and gave him civilian clothes to put on  in four minutes he said i look like any  one of the frenchmen who had rescued me  and my flying suit and equipment had  disappeared  many years after the war a belgian woman  who spent more than three years in a  german concentration camp  for helping the escape of downed allied  flyers  said that she never regretted the heavy  price that she  and other members of the belgian  resistance paid for helping the allied  cause  as she put it the airmen who come for  reunions  feel they can't thank us enough we say  if it wasn't for the english we might be  german now  she was one of the lucky ones who  survived  so as it turned out was dede de jean  after her capture didi had been sent to  a prison in paris  and then to the infamous ravensbruck  concentration camp  the only german camp designed  specifically for women  which was located north of berlin of the  132  000 women and children sent there during  the war  more than 70 percent died of starvation  torture beating hanging shooting  horrific  medical experiments and beginning in  november 1944  in a newly installed gas chamber  deedee was close to dying herself by the  time robin's brook was liberated in the  spring of 1945.  she owed her survival she later said to  the german's  stereotype view of women coming from a  very traditional  patriarchal society they viewed women  chiefly in their conventional  domestic roles as wives and mothers  so even when dede after her capture  freely admitted to creating the comet  line  she managed to avoid execution because  the germans could not  bring themselves to believe that such a  pretty  delicate looking young woman could have  devised such an intricate operation  she later told an interviewer women have  such an  innocent look you know they look so  terribly harmless  germans are not accustomed to women with  opinions of their own  when the war was over a number of  british groups military and civilian  organized annual reunions to celebrate  their wartime bonds with the europeans  with whom they had worked  among those groups was the raf escaping  society  which was sponsored and financed by the  british air ministry  as a way of paying tribute to the  thousands of europeans who had helped  rescue  downed allied airmen the society  provided financial assistance to the  families of escape network members who  had been killed by the germans  and to members requiring medical  treatment or who were otherwise  in need it also helped sponsor reunions  between the airmen  and those who aided them year after year  the most heavily attended gathering  was the reunion celebrating the work of  dede dejong's comet line  didi herself was regarded as a heroine  by the hundreds of britons and americans  whom she and her network had saved  following the war she was awarded the  u.s medal of freedom  and the british george medal both of  them high civilian honors  but she was never comfortable in the  spotlight  for most of the rest of her life she  devoted herself  once again to saving the lives of others  working first as a nurse in a leper  colony in the belgian congo  and then at a leper hospital in ethiopia  but the men she rescued during the war  never forgot her  a few years after the war ended  britain's royal air force  showed its appreciation for what she had  done in a very special way at that time  she was working in the ethiopian capital  of addis ababa she had been told that  her mother was on her deathbed in  belgium  but she had no way to get there quickly  when raf officials learned of the  situation  they ordered a training flight from  africa to britain  to make an unscheduled stop in addis  ababa  the plane picked her up and in another  unscheduled leg of the flight took her  to brussels  after her mother's funeral yet another  raf plane  returned her to ethiopia  from the beginning of her life to the  end didi dejong  was in the words of an airman she saved  one of those rare beings who felt the  misery of the world  and would not let it rest  [Music]  you

