ish frontier  and over the pyrenees she was known  to american and british airmen under her  care as the postmistress  because she referred to them very  affectionately  as her parcels her real identity was  kept a closely guarded secret  from the time of the comet line's  creation in 1941  there had been numerous arrests of its  workers  but deedee herself managed to elude the  nazis for more than 18 months  langley and neve who knew how determined  the germans were to smash the line  were afraid that her luck was running  out  they urged her and her father who worked  closely with her  to come to england before it was too  late  but as always she kept to her own rules  refusing even to consider the thought of  leaving  in early january 1943 didi dejong  set off with three american pilots on  her 18th trip to spain a heavy snowstorm  caught them just before they crossed the  pyrenees  and they were forced to seek shelter for  the night at a safe house in the  foothills of the mountains  the next morning the gestapo acting on a  tip from a local  farm worker burst into the house and  arrested all its occupants  allied airmen were repeatedly warned  that if they were captured  they must not reveal the identities and  locations of those who had hidden or  otherwise helped them  their only obligation they were told was  to give their name  rank and serial number just as if they  had been captured in combat  but the harshness of gestapo  interrogation  proved too much for some including one  of the pilots who was caught with dede  he gave up the names of his guides and  the owners of the safe houses  who had sheltered him while the three  pilots were sent to a pow  camp dede and those who helped her  vanished without a trace  they were among the tens of thousands of  political prisoners in occupied europe  who under a decree from hitler  disappeared into what was  called the knight and fog of nazi  concentration camps  a few weeks later the gestapo again  struck hard at comet  this time arresting nearly 100 of its  operatives in brussels and  elsewhere in belgium before that  happened however  a pretty 28 year old friend and close  associate of deities  named peggy van leer was smuggled out of  belgium  by fellow comet agents and taken to  gibraltar  where she was sent by plane to london  there james langley was waiting to greet  her  he was instantly smitten by the  red-haired blue-eyed belgian  falling in love with her he later told  friends the moment she stepped off the  plane  the two were married the following year  had five children  and lived happily together in suffolk  for the rest of their lives  peggy van leer was lucky to get out when  she did  the first gestapo raid was followed by a  second one  several months later this one triggered  by a french collaborator  working for the gestapo in paris didi  dejong's father  who was head of the line in paris was  rounded up  along with her sister and dozens of  other associates  most of them including paul de jong were  later executed  as james langley later said it seemed  incredible that comet could survive  but survive it did despite the loss of  its founder and so many other key  members  the network was able to rebuild itself  enlisting the help of new couriers and  guides  setting up more safe houses and  maintaining the flow of allied  servicemen back to england  an estimated 3000 civilians mostly  belgians and french  worked with the comet line about 700  were arrested by the germans  and 290 were executed or died in prison  or concentration camps nonetheless  the line was able to continue its rescue  operations until belgium was liberated  in the fall of 1944 of the 800  servicemen  that the comet lines saved in its three  years of existence  118 were escorted to spain  by didi dejong herself  by the end of the war the rescue of  downed allied airmen  had become a commonplace event in all  some seven  thousand british american and other  allied servicemen  were spirited out of occupied europe  during the war  the number of those who helped them  escape has been estimated at more than  12  000 people during the war allied fighter  and bomber crews were briefed on how to  evade capture  and seek out rescuers if they were shot  down  they were given a compass silk maps of  the countries they were flying over  and other emergency supplies to keep  them going while they were looking for  help  if they were shot down they were told  their first objective was to get rid of  their parachute  and leave their landing spot as soon as  possible  as the war progressed those goals became  increasingly easy to achieve  whenever the thunder of planes was heard  in belgium  holland or northern france crowds of  people would gather outside to watch the  planes fly overhead  if parachutes were seen those observers  would hop on their bicycles  or run in the parachute's direction in  the hope of getting there before the  germans  in february 1943 an american pilot shot  down on his way back from a raid on the  french port of san nazaire  was spirited away by several women who  raced up to him  as he landed in august of that year  two american sergeants down near  brussels were each greeted by crowds of  several dozen belgians  and passed on to escape lines that same  month  another u.s sergeant who was shot down  near the french town of toulouse  was swarmed by some 30 frenchmen as he  drifted to the ground  they immediately removed his parachute  and uniform  and gave him civilian clothes to put on  in four minutes he said i look like any  one of the frenchmen who had rescued me  and my flying suit and equipment had  disappeared  many years after the war a belgian woman  who spent more than three years in a  german concentration camp  for helping the escape of downed allied  flyers  said that she never regretted the heavy  price that she  and other members of the belgian  resistance paid for helping the allied  cause  as she put it the airmen who come for  reunions  feel they can't thank us enough we say  if it wasn't for the english we might be  german now  she was one of the lucky ones who  survived  so as it turned out was dede de jean  after her capture didi had been sent to  a prison in paris  and then to the infamous ravensbruck  concentration camp  the only german camp designed  specifically for women  which was located north of berlin of the  132  000 women and children sent there during  the war  more than 70 percent died of starvation  torture beating hanging shooting  horrific  medical experiments and beginning in  november 1944  in a newly installed gas chamber  deedee was close to dying herself by the  time robin's brook was liberated in the  spring of 1945.  she owed her survival she later said to  the german's  stereotype view of women coming from a  very traditional  patriarchal society they viewed women  chiefly in their conventional  domestic roles as wives and mothers  so even when dede after her capture  freely admitted to creating the comet  line  she managed to avoid execution because  the germans could not  bring themselves to believe that such a  pretty  delicate looking young woman could have  devised such an intricate operation  she later told an interviewer women have  such an  innocent look you know they look so  terribly harmless  germans are not accustomed to women with  opinions of their own  when the war was over a number of  british groups military and civilian  organized annual reunions to celebrate  their wartime bonds with the europeans  with whom they had worked  among those groups was the raf escaping  society  which was sponsored and financed by the  british air ministry  as a way of paying tribute to the  thousands of europeans who had helped  rescue  downed allied airmen the society  provided financial assistance to the  families of escape network members who  had been killed by the germans  and to members requiring medical  treatment or who were otherwise  in need it also helped sponsor reunions  between the airmen  and those who aided them year after year  the most heavily attended gathering  was the reunion celebrating the work of  dede dejong's comet line  didi herself was regarded as a heroine  by the hundreds of britons and americans  whom she and her network had saved  following the war she was awarded the  u.s medal of freedom  and the british george medal both of  them high civilian honors  but she was never comfortable in the  spotlight  for most of the rest of her life she  devoted herself  once again to saving the lives of others  working first as a nurse in a leper  colony in the belgian congo  and then at a leper hospital in ethiopia  but the men she rescued during the war  never forgot her  a few years after the war ended  britain's royal air force  showed its appreciation for what she had  done in a very special way at that time  she was working in the ethiopian capital  of addis ababa she had been told that  her mother was on her deathbed in  belgium  but she had no way to get there quickly  when raf officials learned of the  situation  they ordered a training flight from  africa to britain  to make an unscheduled stop in addis  ababa  the plane picked her up and in another  unscheduled leg of the flight took her  to brussels  after her mother's funeral yet another  raf plane  returned her to ethiopia  from the beginning of her life to the  end didi dejong  was in the words of an airman she saved  one of those rare beings who felt the  misery of the world  and would not let it rest  [Music]  you
 

